  

<h2 id="with-7-principles-for-keeping-your-app-data-safe">With 7 principles for keeping your app-data safe</h2>
<p>I love the idea of embracing the tendency to switch tools as a feature, rather than something to be avoided.</p>
<p><a href="https://pkm.social/@msemochkin/117095670269420794" target="_blank">Maxim said</a> —</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I’ve tried keeping tasks directly in Obsidian, using Todoist heavily, classic <span class="small-caps">GTD</span> (as much as that’s actually possible), and the <span class="small-caps">PARA</span> method.<br>
They’re all good in their own way.<br>
At this point, I should probably just admit that every year or year and a half, I simply enjoy switching tools.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There be no shame in app-switching, my friends. If you enjoy it and it’s not a covert procrastination technique you engage in weekly, please do embrace the delight that comes with using a new tool.</p>
<p>When I first wrote that, I felt that the new tool would ideally be used on your locally stored, future-proofed / plain text compatible lists. You know that’s my jam. But reading the comments on this (<a href="https://pkm.social/deck/@ellane/117104132431764019" target="_blank">and another</a>) post woke up the part of my brain not driven by scarcity, or the fear of losing stuff. After all, <a href="https://ellanew.com/2020/08/26/i-was-a-task-management-app-addict" class="wikilink">I was a task management app addict</a> back in the day!</p>
<p>Thanks to the level-headed folk I’m privileged to rub virtual shoulders with online, I now present a more balanced view:</p>
<p><strong>Let your tasks live wherever feels like home to you</strong>. Welcome the process of rewriting (or copy/pasting) them as an opportunity to reevaluate their repeated call on your attention.</p>
<p>So yes, why not switch apps every now and then for the fun of it? As long as it’s not something you’re doing too often, or doing to avoid the things on your todo lists.</p>
<p>As always, I recommend keeping in mind these principles when using any proprietary app:</p>
<ul>
<li>Keep a copy of any task or process-list you would hate to lose, as plain text<br>
</li>
<li>Prefer task apps that allow exporting your lists into plain text. Not to save you time in rewriting them, but as a backup, so you can keep working if your app of choice has a bad day<br>
</li>
<li>Related: Automate daily backups of your most important lists so you don’t have to think about it<br>
</li>
<li>Don’t run your life from any app that needs to be online to work<br>
</li>
<li>Don’t use a Gmail address to log in to any app you rely on, without a well thought out plan B. This week I heard about yet another person losing access to their Gmail account with no warning, and no apparent way to get it back.<br>
</li>
<li>If you’re a pen-and-paper person, use your phone to take photos of your most important pages (it’s quicker than scanning)<br>
</li>
<li>Think of the worst-case scenario, then do today what future you would wish you’d done to mitigate the risks</li>
</ul>
